Here's a JavaScript solution for the problem you're trying to solve:

```javascript
// Create a Set object with five unique elements of different data types
let mySet = new Set([5, "hello", true, { name: "John" }, [1, 2, 3]]);

// Write a function that iterates through the Set and prints out the type of each element
function printElementType(set) {
    set.forEach(element => {
        console.log(typeof element);
    });
}

// Write a method that checks if a passed element exists in the Set and returns a Boolean value
function checkElementExists(set, element) {
    if (element instanceof Set) {
        // If the input is a Set, convert its elements into a string and check if it exists in the original Set
        return set.has(Array.from(element).toString());
    } else {
        return set.has(element);
    }
}

// Call the function and method with the Set
printElementType(mySet);
console.log(checkElementExists(mySet, 5)); // true
console.log(checkElementExists(mySet, "world")); // false
console.log(checkElementExists(mySet, new Set([1, 2, 3]))); // true
```

This script first creates a new Set object `mySet` with five elements(`5`, `"hello"`, `true`, an object `{ name: "John" }`, and an array `[1, 2, 3]`). Then it defines a function `printElementType` which takes a Set as input and prints the type of each element in that Set. The function `checkElementExists` is then defined which checks if an element exists in a Set and returns `true` or `false` accordingly. If the input to `checkElementExists` is a Set, the function converts its elements into a string and checks if this string exists in the original Set. The script ends by calling these functions with `mySet`.
